README-EDITORS -- eight editors that are not vi, and which to takeDOC/README-VI covers the vi family -- two editors, PVIC as `vi' and Elvis --and if you want vi that is where to look.  This file is the rest: SIXEmacsen, a line editor and a binary editor.Short answer: take `beav' if you edit BINARY files, and for Emacs keys take`mg' -- or `umacs' if 45K matters, or `emacs' if you want the macro library.For ordinary text, vi is better served here than anything on this list.THE EIGHT---------  file      bytes  cio  module      source  docs      what it is  ---------------------------------------------------------------------  em        79590   no  em          --      DOC/em    MicroEMACS 3.8b  me       110408   no  me          --      DOC/em    MicroEMACS 3.11  emacs    144910  yes  emacs       --      1         MicroEMACS 4.00  emacs.mm1 131362  no  emacs.mm1   --      --        MicroEMACS 4.00, MM/1  umacs     45412  yes  uMacs       --      DOC/em    uMacs 1.0, small  mg        79126  yes  mg          --      3 files   MicroGnuEmacs  ed        85810  yes  ed          yes     7 files   GNU ed 0.2, line editor  beav      85950   no  beav        yes     1         BINARY editorA star in DOC/INDEX means the program needs Microware's `cio'.  It shipshere, so on this disk that costs nothing; it is one more file to carry ifyou take the editor away.  DOC/README-CIO.SIX EMACSEN, AND FIVE OF THEM ARE ONE PROGRAM---------------------------------------------This is the question this file exists for, so here it is plainly.  Five ofthe six are MicroEMACS -- Dave Conroy's editor as continued by DanielLawrence -- at four different versions and two different sizes:    em          3.8b      the oldest here    me          3.11      German messages in places; ported by Knut Grunwald    emacs       4.00      the newest and the biggest; reads a macro library    emacs.mm1   4.00      the same 4.00, built for the MM/1    umacs       1.0       uMacs, a cut-down MicroEMACS in a third the spaceThe sixth, `mg', is NOT MicroEMACS.  It is Mg -- MicroGnuEmacs -- written tobehave like GNU Emacs rather than like MicroEMACS: the key bindings followGNU's, and there is no macro language at all.  If you know GNU Emacs andwant the muscle memory to work, `mg' is the one; if you know MicroEMACS,any of the other five will feel right.So the choice is really three questions:  Do you want GNU Emacs bindings?                 mg  Do you want the macro library and the newest    MicroEMACS, and can you carry 145K + USR/LIB    /EMACS (eighteen files)?                      emacs  Do you want the smallest thing with Emacs keys? umacs, 45K`em' and `me' are older MicroEMACS versions kept because they are whatarrived; neither has source here, DOC/em is a short page for both, andneither does anything the three above do not.  `emacs.mm1' is for anMM/1 -- take it only if that is your machine, and note it needs no`cio' where plain `emacs' does.What each wants beside itself:    emacs       USR/LIB/EMACS -- eighteen macro files, a `.rc' and a help                text.  They ship.    emacs.mm1   the same, and it looks for them under a compiled-in                /h0/memacs400/cmd as well    umacs       SYS/umacs.hlp    mg          nothing; DOC/mg is its documentationTAKE THIS IF------------  beav    you edit BINARY.  It is the only thing on this disk that edits a          file as bytes with a proper screen -- `hexed' dumps to a text          editor and back, which is a different idea -- and its source is          here.  Needs no Microware module.  Type hex digits to change the          byte under the cursor; Ctl-C then y leaves without saving.  mg      you want Emacs key bindings in 79K with no macro language and          three files of documentation.  emacs   you want the macros.  145K plus USR/LIB/EMACS.  umacs   you want Emacs bindings in 45K.  Its module is called `uMacs'.  ed      you are writing a script that edits a file without a screen.          BUT: GNU ed 0.2 makes its temporary file at `/r0', so mount a RAM          disk first (`mount -r=256k /r0') or it stops at once.  em, me  a short page in DOC/em and no source; try them only if the above do          not suit.IF YOU CAN TAKE ONLY ONE FILEFor text: vi -- see DOC/README-VI.For binary: `beav', 85,950 bytes, needs no Microware module.THE HAZARD IF YOU TAKE SEVERAL`umacs' reports its module as `uMacs'.  OS-9's name comparison iscase-insensitive, so `uMacs' and a hypothetical `umacs' would collide --they do not here, because nothing else claims either.  Every other editorin this list reports its own filename.  `tools/module_census.py' listsevery name on the disk that differs from its file.SEDT is gone.  `sedt', `e' and `new_e' were three builds of it and came offthe disk on 2026-09-18; SOURCES.txt says why.